{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades a powerful and often shrewd ruler who navigated complex political landscapes. His reign was marked by military campaigns diplomatic maneuvering and economic development within his territory.\n\n*Early Life and Accession* Raymond was born around 1110 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Hauteville. He inherited the County upon his father's death in 1154. His early years were characterized by consolidating his power dealing with internal rebellions and maintaining relations with neighboring rulers. He faced challenges from ambitious vassals and rival dynasties seeking to expand their influence in the Levant. He demonstrated military skill and political acumen from the beginning establishing himself as a capable leader.\n\nRaymond III's reign saw significant military activity. He engaged in conflicts with the Seljuk Turks and other Muslim powers defending Tripoli's borders and expanding its territory. He participated in Crusades including the Third Crusade playing a crucial role in the defense of Jerusalem. He allied himself with other Crusader states forming strategic partnerships to counter Muslim aggression. He also invested in fortifications strengthening Tripoli's defenses against potential invaders.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances* A key aspect of Raymond III's rule was his skillful diplomacy. He forged alliances with various powers including the Kingdom of Sicily the Holy Roman Empire and other Crusader states. These alliances provided military support economic assistance and diplomatic leverage. He understood the importance of maintaining a balance of power in the region and skillfully navigated the complex web of relationships between different political entities. His diplomatic efforts helped to secure Tripoli's position as a major player in the Levant.\n\nRaymond III fostered economic growth within the County of Tripoli. He encouraged trade promoted agriculture and invested in infrastructure. Tripoli became a prosperous center of commerce attracting merchants from across the Mediterranean. He also supported the development of religious institutions and promoted cultural activities. His policies contributed to the stability and prosperity of his territory.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III died in 1199 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV. He is remembered as one of the most successful and influential Counts of Tripoli. His reign was a period of stability prosperity and military strength for the County. He left a legacy of strong leadership diplomatic skill and military prowess. He played a vital role in the Crusades and helped to shape the political landscape of the Levant during the 12th and 13th centuries. His policies and actions had a lasting impact on the history of Tripoli and the Crusader states.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0011152960360050201}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2664777338504791}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.12502680718898773}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.08393791317939758}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00027021096320822835}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.6702524337451905e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -6.556489552167477e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.1709168979432434e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.0023934785276651382}, {"token": "▁until", "logprob": -0.026323901489377022}, {"token": "▁his", "logprob": -0.0003110878460574895}, {"token": "▁death", "logprob": -1.6689160474925302e-05}, {"token": "▁in", "